<%# A clock time, rendered by the reader's own browser. #397.

    This is the first time-of-day anywhere in the app, and it lands directly on top of the
    defect #349 names: there is no timezone, not per account and not in config, so the server
    is UTC and every naive stamp in the database is UTC. Printing one straight would tell a
    lifter in California that their six o'clock session started at one in the morning -- which
    is not a small wrongness on a page whose whole subject is when you trained.

    So the instant goes over the wire and the browser formats it. That is correct today for
    every reader wherever they are, it needs no configuration, and it does not pre-empt #349:
    when an account grows a timezone, this keeps working and the *server's* idea of "today"
    is the thing that gets fixed there. It is also the trick the session clock already uses --
    the server sends a number and the browser turns it into something to read.

    data-local is epoch seconds, which is unambiguous. The stamp is stored naive and written
    by this process, so Ruby reads it back in the same zone it was written in and `to_i` is
    the true instant regardless of what that zone is.

    The <time> element carries the same instant in datetime, offset and all, so the page is
    machine-readable whether or not any script runs.

    **The fallback says UTC out loud.** With JavaScript off the visible text is the UTC
    reading, labelled, rather than a bare number that looks local and is not. An unlabelled
    wrong clock time is worse than an awkward right one -- and unlike the session clock's
    fallback, which is an elapsed count and therefore zone-free, this one cannot be made
    honest by leaving the label off. %>
<time data-local="<%= at.to_i %>"
      datetime="<%= at.strftime('%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S%:z') %>"><%= at.utc.strftime('%H:%M UTC') %></time>
